% ============================================================================
% voting-requirement-exception — the carve-out USA.gov states for each U.S.
% voting requirement (adj-facts-stdlib, CIVICS).
% ============================================================================
% "You must be registered to vote" is the kind of sentence a summary
% produces and a source rarely says without qualification. USA.gov lists
% four requirements for voting in U.S. federal, state, and local
% elections — and attaches to EACH one a stated exception that a summary
% would flatten away. This table holds the exceptions, which is the half
% that gets lost. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? voting_requirement_exception(voter_registration_by_state_deadline, $E)
%       % north_dakota_does_not_require_registration
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `voting_requirement_exception(requirement, exception)` carrying
% the source citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the carve-out WITH its source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls, and abstains on any requirement not one of these
% four. The query also runs BACKWARD:
%
%     ? voting_requirement_exception($R, experiencing_homelessness_still_meets_it)
%       % state_residency
%
% WHY THIS AXIS AND NOT THE REQUIREMENTS THEMSELVES. The four requirements
% are a flat bulleted list — citizenship, residency, age, registration —
% with no second column the source states, and this stdlib's own rule is
% to match the shape of the SOURCE rather than force a familiar table
% shape onto it (see `bill-stage-successor.adj`'s header for the case that
% established it). A `requirement -> description` table would have had to
% paraphrase each bullet into a description the page never separately
% states. The EXCEPTIONS are different: the page attaches exactly one
% stated carve-out to each of the four bullets, so requirement -> exception
% is a relation the source genuinely supplies on both sides, uniformly,
% with nothing invented.
%
% It is also the more useful half. An LLM asked "do you have to register
% to vote?" will confidently say yes; the fact that North Dakota does not
% require registration at all is precisely the sort of detail that
% disappears into a confident summary. Recalling the exception WITH its
% citation is the behaviour this stdlib exists to make possible.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from USA.gov's "Who can and cannot vote"
% page. Each row decodes the carve-out the page attaches to one bullet of
% its own "You can vote in U.S. federal, state, and local elections if
% you:" list:
%
%     requirement (this table's atom)             exception (this table's atom)                          quote (verbatim)
%     -----------------------------------------   ----------------------------------------------------   ----------------------------------------------
%     us_citizenship                              non_citizens_may_vote_in_some_local_elections_only     "some areas allow non-citizens to vote in
%                                                                                                           local elections only"
%     state_residency                             experiencing_homelessness_still_meets_it               "You can be experiencing homelessness and
%                                                                                                           still meet these requirements."
%     age_eighteen_by_election_day                some_states_allow_seventeen_year_olds_in_primaries     "Some states allow 17-year-olds who will be
%                                                                                                           18 by Election Day to vote in primaries."
%     voter_registration_by_state_deadline        north_dakota_does_not_require_registration             "North Dakota does not require voter
%                                                                                                           registration."
%
% curl-fetched the raw page and read it byte-for-byte before writing this
% file (not just an AI-summarized WebFetch); the URL was reachability-
% checked from this machine before any scoping work. `trust authoritative`
% — USA.gov is the U.S. General Services Administration's official guide to
% government information, a first-party .gov publisher of exactly the fact
% being recalled, the same tier the seven sibling civics libraries cite.
%
% Honest abstention:
%
%   - The page's separate "Who cannot vote?" section — non-citizens
%     including permanent legal residents, some people convicted of a
%     felony, some people with a mental disability, and U.S. citizens
%     residing in U.S. territories (who cannot vote for president in the
%     general election) — states DISQUALIFICATIONS, not exceptions to a
%     requirement. That is a different relation entirely and belongs in its
%     own table; folding it in here would blur "you still qualify despite
%     X" together with "you do not qualify because of X", which are
%     opposite claims.
%   - The registration bullet's own "In almost every state, you can
%     register to vote before you turn 18 if you will be 18 by Election
%     Day" is about WHEN you may register, not an exception to whether
%     registration is required, and the age bullet's primaries carve-out is
%     the one this table records for age.
%   - A requirement not one of these four — and there are none others in
%     the source's list — abstains rather than being invented.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table voting_requirement_exception {
    columns requirement, exception

    row (us_citizenship, non_citizens_may_vote_in_some_local_elections_only)
    row (state_residency, experiencing_homelessness_still_meets_it)
    row (age_eighteen_by_election_day, some_states_allow_seventeen_year_olds_in_primaries)
    row (voter_registration_by_state_deadline, north_dakota_does_not_require_registration)

    source "North Dakota does not require voter registration."
    locator "https://www.usa.gov/who-can-vote"
    trust authoritative

    cites "some areas allow non-citizens to vote in local elections only" locator "https://www.usa.gov/who-can-vote"
    cites "You can be experiencing homelessness and still meet these requirements." locator "https://www.usa.gov/who-can-vote"
    cites "Some states allow 17-year-olds who will be 18 by Election Day to vote in primaries." locator "https://www.usa.gov/who-can-vote"
}
